<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.avoforecast.kalshi-evolve</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Users/hermesagent/miniconda3/bin/uv</string>
        <string>run</string>
        <string>python</string>
        <string>-m</string>
        <string>avo.cli</string>
        <string>evolve</string>
        <string>kalshi_quant</string>
        <string>--model</string>
        <string>sonnet</string>
        <string>-n</string>
        <string>2</string>
        <string>--generations</string>
        <string>1</string>
        <string>--run-id</string>
        <string>kalshi_quant-20260905T044920Z</string>
    </array>

    <!-- --run-id is PINNED, and the job is wrong without it. `run_id =
         args.run_id or new_run_id(...)`, so an unpinned scheduled run creates
         a FRESH run directory every night; pool_changed() then finds no prior
         generation, reports "first generation", and generates unconditionally.
         The cadence gate would be silently defeated -- the exact failure class
         this README warns about, where the job runs, exits 0, and does the
         wrong work. If the run is ever restarted, this string must change with
         it; `ls runs/` is the check.

         --model is PINNED for the reasons in the research plist: the CLI
         default is a user setting, exhausted Fable credits killed a whole
         generation silently (2026-09-11), and INVARIANT #7 forbids mixing
         backends within a run. Sonnet 5 because it GENERATES better per unit
         cost and wall clock -- measured 2026-09-11, two accepted candidates in
         ~17 min each against Opus's one in 27.

         -n 2, not more. A generation run is a full agentic session, not a
         request, and it competes with the human's own rolling window.
         Generations 3 and 4 ran -n 8 and burned fourteen invocations against
         an exhausted quota. -->

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/hermesagent/.local/bin:/Users/hermesagent/miniconda3/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/hermesagent/development/avo-forecast</string>

    <!-- 03:35 daily. Scheduling this only became safe on 2026-09-20, when
         pool_changed() started requiring a new candidate or a moved verdict:
         before it, a nightly run would have spent a full agentic session
         re-deriving identical parents, which is what generations 3 and 4 did
         by hand.

         03:35 because the hourly FULL sweep runs :07-:33 and the heavy half of
         evolve is its scoring pass, which lands in the :35-:00 gap. Overnight
         because a generation competes with the human's own quota window.

         A SKIPPED run is not free but it is cheap in the currency that
         matters: ~25 min of local CPU for the scoring pass (score_all must run
         before the pool can be compared -- you cannot know a verdict moved
         without recomputing it) and ZERO API quota. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key><integer>3</integer>
        <key>Minute</key><integer>35</integer>
    </dict>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/hermesagent/development/avo-forecast/data/evolve.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/hermesagent/development/avo-forecast/data/evolve.log</string>

    <!-- FALSE, unlike the capture jobs. Those want to start the moment they
         are loaded; this one would fire a generation on install, possibly on
         top of a manual run in progress. _EvolveLock would catch that, but a
         job whose first act is to collide is a bad job. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
